A||N||3||Daniel87GT||Vented brakes on an 87||||5||
Z||000000||Daniel87GT||11-10-2000||01:02 PM||||I recently bought an blue 87 GT.  I love it.  Unfortunately my 85 2M4 got jealous and broke its timing chain the next day.  Anybody want some parts/a project car?<P>Anyway, the 87 had some problems with the calipers on the front brakes.  One wouldn't work & the other was not retracting.  I got to looking at them and wow! vented rotors.  I know enough about fieros to know that vented rotors are not stock.  Needless to say, I had a fun time finding new calipers at the parts store.  Eventually I found a visually perfect match to a set of stock mid-80's Monte Carlo calipers.  Would these brakes be the same as the 88 Fiero brakes?<P>Daniel Shelley||||reg||5
Z||000001||Will||11-10-2000||03:41 PM||||Those are probably the Grand-Am/Beretta rotors. Are the rotors a separate piece from the hub? If the rotors come off the hub, then that is most likely what the previous owner did to your car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Daniel87GT||11-10-2000||04:56 PM||||Maybe the rotors are from a Grand-Am, but the calipers are definately not.  After all the discussion in the forums, calipers for the Grand-Am were the first ones that I looked at.  They were visibly different.<P>The rotors are a seperate piece from the hub.  It doesn't seem logical to me though to not do a matching set with the rotors & the calipers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||11-10-2000||09:50 PM||||What is the diameter of the rotors? Thickness? Is there an adapter used to mount the caliper? Show us some pics! Please! Sounds like maybe the Chrysler rotor/Camaro caliper swap.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
